1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,018
This video shows how to turn on the Omnichannel CRM:

2
00:00:04,018 --> 00:00:07,816
why it keeps leads apart from your contacts, how to run the starter

3
00:00:07,816 --> 00:00:11,822
recipe, and how to set up the lead statuses, the opportunity stages and

4
00:00:11,822 --> 00:00:14,580
the lead sources that your team will work with.

5
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:23,422
A purchased list, a trade-show scan or a web form gives you prospects.

6
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:27,720
They are not customers yet, and they should not fill your contact list.

7
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:32,680
Teams also need to know how far each prospect has come, which ones

8
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:35,720
qualified, and which deals are worth how much.

9
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:41,131
The Omnichannel CRM keeps prospects as leads, lets you convert the ones that

10
00:00:41,131 --> 00:00:46,360
qualify into contacts, and groups contacts and deals under accounts and opportunities.

11
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:52,454
Statuses, stages and sources are lists that you control, so the CRM follows

12
00:00:52,454 --> 00:00:54,170
your way of selling.

13
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:02,609
A lead is a prospect you can call and text like a contact.

14
00:01:02,609 --> 00:01:07,180
An account is the company or household that contacts belong to.

15
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:11,612
An opportunity is a deal you are working on, with a stage, an

16
00:01:11,612 --> 00:01:13,260
amount and a close date.

17
00:01:14,010 --> 00:01:17,985
Lead statuses say where a lead is in qualification.

18
00:01:17,985 --> 00:01:21,122
Opportunity stages say where a deal is.

19
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:24,330
Lead sources say where a lead came from.

20
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,975
The starter recipe sets all of this up, and you can rename or

21
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:31,010
extend every type afterwards.

22
00:01:35,223 --> 00:01:38,335
Open Tools, Recipes and search for CRM.

23
00:01:38,335 --> 00:01:42,968
The Omnichannel CRM starter recipe describes what it creates:

24
00:01:42,968 --> 00:01:48,085
a Lead type, a Contact type, a Sales Opportunity type, and a starter

25
00:01:48,085 --> 00:01:49,883
set of lead sources.

26
00:01:50,337 --> 00:01:52,347
Click Run and confirm.

27
00:01:52,347 --> 00:01:57,587
The recipe turns the Omnichannel CRM feature on and adds the types.

28
00:01:58,038 --> 00:02:03,325
When it is done, the Interaction Center menu has Leads, Accounts and Opportunities,

29
00:02:03,325 --> 00:02:08,018
with Lead Statuses, Lead Sources and Opportunity Stages under Management.

30
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:16,430
Open Interaction Center, Management, Lead Statuses.

31
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,196
Each status has a name, an order, and a type:

32
00:02:20,196 --> 00:02:22,560
Open, Closed or Converted.

33
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:26,469
Leads in an Open status are still worked and loaded.

34
00:02:26,469 --> 00:02:30,342
Closed means the lead was finished without being converted.

35
00:02:30,342 --> 00:02:34,393
Converted is the status a lead gets when it becomes a contact.

36
00:02:34,847 --> 00:02:36,319
Click Add Status.

37
00:02:36,319 --> 00:02:41,119
Give it a Name and a Description, set the Order, and choose the Type.

38
00:02:41,119 --> 00:02:45,279
Only an open status can be the one where a new lead starts.

39
00:02:45,279 --> 00:02:46,367
Click Save.

40
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:55,275
Open Interaction Center, Management, Opportunity Stages.

41
00:02:55,275 --> 00:03:00,360
A stage has a name, an order and a probability from 0 to 100.

42
00:03:00,812 --> 00:03:05,397
Closed says the opportunity is finished, and Won says it was a success.

43
00:03:05,397 --> 00:03:08,135
A stage can only be won if it is closed.

44
00:03:08,135 --> 00:03:11,112
The probability feeds the weighted pipeline.

45
00:03:11,564 --> 00:03:17,051
Click Add Stage, enter the name, the order and the probability, and click Save.

46
00:03:17,051 --> 00:03:20,924
A new opportunity starts with its stage's probability.

47
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:29,009
Open Interaction Center, Management, Lead Sources.

48
00:03:29,009 --> 00:03:36,460
The starter recipe added Web form, Referral, Trade show, Purchased list, Advertisement and Cold call.

49
00:03:36,913 --> 00:03:40,005
A lead source is an ordinary content item.

50
00:03:40,005 --> 00:03:44,257
Click New to add one, such as Partner event, and publish it.

51
00:03:44,257 --> 00:03:50,763
It then appears in the lead source pickers of leads, opportunities, activity loads and imports.

52
00:03:54,947 --> 00:04:00,046
Open Design, Content Definition, Content Types and edit the Lead type.

53
00:04:00,046 --> 00:04:02,797
The Lead part has its own settings.

54
00:04:03,249 --> 00:04:08,342
Choose the contact type that a converted lead becomes, and the opportunity type

55
00:04:08,342 --> 00:04:10,019
that is offered first.

56
00:04:10,469 --> 00:04:13,648
An opportunity type has the Opportunity part.

57
00:04:13,648 --> 00:04:17,325
In its settings you choose the stages that type uses.

58
00:04:17,325 --> 00:04:20,379
If you pick none, every stage is available.

59
00:04:24,579 --> 00:04:29,959
Work a purchased list as leads, and keep your contact list for real customers.

60
00:04:30,709 --> 00:04:35,729
Track trade-show leads by source, and see which source converts best.

61
00:04:36,479 --> 00:04:41,229
Follow every deal through its stages, with a probability for the pipeline.

62
00:04:41,979 --> 00:04:47,929
Use different opportunity types for new sales and renewals, each with its own stages.

63
00:04:49,929 --> 00:04:54,994
Run the starter recipe, adjust the statuses, stages and sources, and your team

64
00:04:54,994 --> 00:04:58,492
can start working leads, accounts and opportunities.

65
00:04:58,492 --> 00:05:00,059
Thanks for watching.

